The battery pack stores and supplies electrical energy required for vehicle propulsion and other systems(e. g. heating). It provides high-voltage power to the electric motor and supports low-voltage systems via a DC-DC converter. The battery pack manages charging and discharging operations and communicates with other ECUs to report its state of charge (SoC), temperature, and health status. It receives energy from regenerative braking and controls thermal conditions through its integrated thermal management system. The battery pack also ensures operational safety by monitoring voltage, current, and temperature of individual cells, and can disconnect the cells/modules from the system in case of faults.

NOTE
Regarding the basic operation of storing and supplying energy, the battery pack functions independently, but coordination with other ECUs (e.g., VCU, charger ECU) is required for vehicle-level energy management and safety.

EV battery pack operational environment

The item (EV battery pack) is connected with the gateway ECU through BMS, and the gateway ECU is connected with the Cloud by data communication.

Cloud has external communication interfaces: - Internet. Assumption: - Cloud doesn’t accept external commands from other Internet users other than the EV gateway ECU.

Gateway ECU has external communication interfaces:  — OBD-II.  Assumption:  — gateway ECU has strong security controls including a firewall function'),
    ('9c159075-4c6a-5723-8471-5e5ba4f0740f', '7045b747-acd4-5124-888e-1c29ae0bd42c', 'Battery pack (Second life)', 'ITEM', NULL, 'The stationary ESS functions as a source of electrical energy for stationary applications such as grid support, load balancing, or backup power. It stores electrical energy and supplies it to connected systems when needed, supporting both high- and low-voltage outputs depending on system requirements. The ESS manages its charging and discharging operations and communicates with external controllers to report key operational parameters such as SoC, temperature, and system health. It may receive energy from sources such as the electrical grid or renewable generation (e.g., solar panels) and maintains safe operating conditions through the BMS. To ensure operational safety, the ESS continuously monitors voltage, current, and temperature at the cell or module level and can isolate individual units if abnormal conditions are detected. While the ESS performs core energy storage and delivery functions autonomously, it typically operates in coordination with external systems such as EMS. On the one hand, the EMS communicates with the BMS using a CAN connection. On the other hand, the EMS communicates with the Cloud through a secure data communication link. The Cloud has an external interface to the Internet; however, it is assumed that only authorized communication from the EMS is accepted, and no external commands from other Internet sources are processed. The EMS may also include external interfaces such as remote diagnostic ports and is assumed to have strong cybersecurity controls, including firewall functionality.'),
